WEBVTT

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:04.865
What is Ohm's law? Why tired cells dim your torch.

00:00:05.035 --> 00:00:09.545
Voltage pushes, resistance holds back, current flows.

00:00:09.715 --> 00:00:13.585
The flow keeps pace with the push, at constant temperature.

00:00:14.505 --> 00:00:18.415
Two volts, ten ohms: the meter reads point two.

00:00:18.935 --> 00:00:23.585
Double the push: four volts. The meter climbs to point four.

00:00:24.075 --> 00:00:29.098
Voltage equals current times resistance; the hold-back stays ten ohms.

00:00:29.988 --> 00:00:33.178
So does it always double? Watch a bulb.

00:00:33.318 --> 00:00:40.728
Its filament heats, resistance rises: the flow falls short. So the rule needs a steady temperature.

00:00:41.218 --> 00:00:43.793
Keep it cool, and the rule holds.

00:00:43.963 --> 00:00:49.953
Back to the resistor: six volts, the meter lands on point six, right on the rule.

00:00:50.473 --> 00:00:52.793
Twice the push, twice the flow.

00:00:53.283 --> 00:00:55.938
Try the free lesson on Ohm's law.

00:00:56.078 --> 00:00:58.188
Professor Diki Simplified.
